<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hohenzollern Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Burg Hohenzollern</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hohenzollern Castle is a hilltop castle on Mount Hohenzollern near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hechingen">Hechingen</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, the ancestral seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Hohenzollern dynasty</a> that later ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>. The first castle was destroyed by the Swabian imperial cities in 1423 and rebuilt; the present Gothic Revival building was constructed in 1850–1867 for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-iv-of-prussia">Frederick William IV of Prussia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hohenzollern-castle.jpg" alt="Hohenzollern Castle" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A. Kniesel (= -donald-), Lauffen · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burg_Hohenzollern_ak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The castle on Mount Hohenzollern is the third of three hilltop castles built on the site. Its name derives from Söller, meaning terrace, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> solarium. The first castle was built in the 11th century by the Counts of Zollern. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a> is first mentioned in 1061, and the castle itself under the name Castro Zolre in 1267. It remained with the Swabian branch of the family, the dynastic seniors of the Franconian-Brandenburgian branch that later acquired its own imperial throne.</p><p>The second castle, larger and sturdier, was begun in 1454 and served as a refuge for the Catholic Swabian Hohenzollerns, including during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. Württemberg troops captured it in 1634, and it then came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> control for about a century. French soldiers occupied it in the winter of 1744/45. After the last Austrian owner left in 1798 it fell into ruin, and by the beginning of the 19th century only the Chapel of St Michael remained usable.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The present castle was the work of Crown Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-iv-of-prussia">Frederick William IV of Prussia</a>, who climbed the mountain in 1819 on his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to learn about his family's roots. As king he wrote in 1844 of his wish to see the castle made habitable again. He engaged the architect Friedrich August Stüler, whose design of 1846 drew on English Gothic Revival architecture and the châteaux of the Loire Valley. Construction began in 1850 and was completed on 3 October 1867 under Frederick William's brother, King William I, funded by the Brandenburg-Prussian and Hohenzollern-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmaringen">Sigmaringen</a> lines of the family.</p><p>The castle covers almost the whole summit. The Eagle Gate and its drawbridge form the entrance, and the palatial buildings stand on the outline of the second castle. The Count's Hall has rib vaulting supported by eight free-standing red marble columns, with grisailles by Stüler depicting the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a>. Like Neuschwanstein, it is a monument to German Romanticism and an idealised vision of a medieval knight's castle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After its completion the castle was not regularly occupied but used mainly as a showpiece, and none of the Hohenzollern emperors lived there. The last Prussian Crown Prince, Wilhelm, stayed there for several months after fleeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> ahead of Soviet forces in 1945, and he and his wife Cecilie are buried there. From 1952 Prince Louis Ferdinand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> added artworks and memorabilia, among them the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> and a uniform of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a>. An earthquake on 3 September 1978 caused some turrets to collapse, and repairs lasted until the mid-1990s. The castle is privately owned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hohenzollern Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The present <b>Hohenzollern Castle</b> is the third on its hilltop: the medieval fortress was razed by the Swabian imperial cities in 1423, and its successor was rebuilt in the 1850s–1860s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
